title: Memory: the clock
check: behaves
cycles: 100
mode: vhdl
chapter: Memory
---- intro
Everything so far has had no memory: the outputs follow the inputs at once. To remember, a circuit needs a **clock**, a signal that ticks 0, 1, 0, 1, and a **flip-flop**, which copies its input to its output at the moment the clock goes from 0 to 1 (the **rising edge**) and keeps it until the next one.

In VHDL, what happens on a clock edge goes in a **process**:

```
process (clk)
begin
    if rising_edge(clk) then
        q <= d;
    end if;
end process;
```

Press **Run** above the circuit to start its clock, or **Step** to tick it once.
---- task
Make `q` take the value of `d` on every rising edge of `clk`, and keep it in between.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity dff is
  port(
    clk : in  std_logic;
    d   : in  std_logic;
    q   : out std_logic
  );
end dff;

architecture rtl of dff is
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity dff is
  port(
    clk : in  std_logic;
    d   : in  std_logic;
    q   : out std_logic
  );
end dff;

architecture rtl of dff is
begin
    process (clk)
    begin
        if rising_edge(clk) then
            q <= d;
        end if;
    end process;
end rtl;
---- hint1
Copy the process from the text above into the architecture, between `begin` and `end rtl;`.
---- hint2
The `q <= d;` goes inside the `if rising_edge(clk) then ... end if;`, which goes inside `process (clk) begin ... end process;`.
---- solution
```
process (clk)
begin
    if rising_edge(clk) then
        q <= d;
    end if;
end process;
```
Flip `d` while the clock is stopped: `q` does not change until the next Step.
---- done
That is memory: `q` holds its value between clock edges, whatever `d` does. Counters, registers, state machines and whole processors are built from flip-flops like this one. Next: many of them, counting.
